questions.corrige_markdown:
**(a)** $P \subset M$: every student who studies Physics also studies Mathematics (the set of Physics students is a subset of the set of Mathematics students).

**(b)** 25 students study all three subjects: Mathematics, Physics and Chemistry.

**(c)** $n(M \cap P \cap C') = 15$.

**(d)** $n(C \cap M' \cap P') = 7$.

**(e)** Because $P \subset M$, the circle for $P$ is drawn entirely inside the circle for $M$, and the circle for $C$ overlaps both. Fill in the regions:

- all three subjects: $25$ (given in (b));
- $M$ and $P$ but not $C$: $15$ (given in (c));
- $C$ only: $7$ (given in (d));
- $P$ only, or $P$ and $C$ but not $M$: $0$, because $P \subset M$;
- $M$ and $C$ but not $P$: $n(C) - 25 - 7 = 37 - 32 = 5$;
- $M$ only: $n(M) - 25 - 15 - 5 = 53 - 45 = 8$.

Check: $n(M) = 8 + 15 + 25 + 5 = 53$ and $n(C) = 25 + 5 + 7 = 37$. The class has $8 + 15 + 25 + 5 + 7 = 60$ students studying at least one of the three subjects. *(Venn diagram to be drawn by the candidate; the universal set $\mathcal{E}$ is the box around the three circles.)*
